Let us now assume that for a particular value of $n$

$$\begin{align*}
Y_{n}=A_{n .0} \Sigma\left(\mu^{n}\right) & +A_{n .1} \Sigma\left(\mu^{n-2} \lambda^{1}\right)+\text { \&c. } \\
& +A_{n .,} \Sigma\left(\mu^{n-2} \lambda^{n}\right)+\text { \&c. } \tag{34}
\end{align*}$$

where the $A$ 's are numerical coefficients. We may write the
 series in the abbreviated form

$$\begin{equation*}
Y_{n}=S\left[A_{n \cdot n} \Sigma\left(\mu^{n-2 s} \lambda^{s}\right)\right], \tag{35}
\end{equation*}$$

where $S$ indicates a summation in which all values of 8 , including
 zero, not greater than $\frac{1}{2} n$, are to be taken.


To obtain the corresponding solid harmonic of negative degree
 $(n+1)$ and order $n$, we multiply by $r^{-(n+1)}$, and obtain

$$\begin{equation*}
V_{n}=S\left[A_{n . s} r^{2 .-2 n-1} \Sigma\left(p^{n-2 s} \lambda^{*}\right)\right] \tag{36}
\end{equation*}$$

putting $r \mu=p$, as in equation (3).

If we differentiate $V_{n}$ with respect to a new axis $h_{m}$ we obtain
 $-(n+1) V_{n+1}$, and therefore

$$\begin{align*}
&(n+1) V_{n+1}=S\left[A_{n . s}(2 n+1-2 s) r^{2 s-2 n-3} \Sigma\left(p_{m}^{n-2 s+1} \lambda^{s}\right)\right. \\
&\left.-A_{n . s} r^{2 s-2 n-1} \Sigma\left(p^{n-2 s-1} \lambda_{m}^{s+1}\right)\right] . \tag{37}
\end{align*}$$


If we wish to obtain the terms containing 8 cosines with
 double suffixes, we must diminish $s$ by unity in the last term,
 and we find

$$\begin{align*}
(n+1) V_{n+1}=S\left[r ^ { 2 s - 2 n - 3 } \left\{A_{n . s}(2 n\right.\right. & -2 s+1) \Sigma\left(p_{n}^{n-2 s+1} \Lambda^{s}\right) \\
& \left.-A_{n . s-1} \Sigma\left(p^{n-2 s+1} \lambda_{m}^{s}\right) \dagger\right] \tag{38}
\end{align*}$$


Now the two classes of products are not distinguished from
 each other in any way except that the suffix $m$ occurs among
 the $p$ 's in one and among the $\lambda$ 's in the other. Hence their
 coefficients must be the same, and since we ought to be able to
 obtain the same result by putting $n+1$ for $n$ in the expression
 for $V_{n}$ and multiplying by $n+1$, we obtain the following equa
tions,

$$\begin{equation*}
(n+1) A_{n+1 \cdot s}=(2 n-28+1) A_{n \cdot s}=-A_{n \cdot s-1} . \tag{39}
\end{equation*}$$


If we put $s=0$, we obtain

$$\begin{equation*}
(n+1) A_{n+1 \cdot 0}=(2 n+1) A_{n \cdot 0} ; \tag{40}
\end{equation*}$$

and therefore, since $A_{1 \cdot 0}=1$,

$$\begin{equation*}
A_{n, 0}=\frac{2 n!}{2^{n}(n!)^{2}} \tag{41}
\end{equation*}$$

and from this we obtain the general value of the coefficient

$$\begin{equation*}
A_{n, s}=(-1)^{\cdot} \frac{(2 n-2 s)!}{2^{n-s} n!(n-s)} \tag{+2}
\end{equation*}$$

and finally the trigonometrical expression for the surface har-

$$\left(e^{i a} \frac{d}{d \xi}+e^{-i a} \frac{d}{d \eta}\right)\left(e^{i\left(a+\frac{2 \pi}{\sigma}\right)} \frac{d}{d \xi}+e^{-i\left(a+\frac{2 \pi}{\sigma}\right)} \frac{d}{d \eta}\right)\left(e^{i\left(a+\frac{4 \pi}{\sigma}\right)} \frac{d}{d \xi}+e^{-i\left(a+\frac{4 \pi}{\sigma}\right)} \frac{d}{d \eta}\right) \ldots$$


This equals

$$\begin{equation*}
\cos \sigma a\left\{\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \xi^{\sigma}}+\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \eta^{\sigma}}\right\}+\sin \sigma a . i\left\{\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \xi^{\sigma}}-\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \eta^{\sigma}}\right\} . \tag{58}
\end{equation*}$$


If $\sigma$ is even we may prove that the operation of differentiating
 may be written

$$\begin{equation*}
(-1)^{\frac{\sigma+2}{2}}\left\{\cos \sigma a \cdot i\left(\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \xi^{\sigma}}-\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \eta^{\sigma}}\right)-\sin \sigma a\left(\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \xi^{\sigma}}+\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \eta^{\sigma}}\right)\right\} . \tag{59}
\end{equation*}$$


Thus, if $i\left(\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \xi^{\sigma}}-\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \eta^{\sigma}}\right)=D_{s,}^{(\sigma)}, \quad \frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \xi^{\sigma}}+\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d \eta^{\sigma}}=D_{c}^{(\sigma)}$,

we may express the operation of differentiating with respect to the
 $\sigma$ axes in terms of $D_{s,}^{(\sigma)} D_{c}^{(\sigma)}$. These are, of course, real operations,
 and may be expressed without the use of imaginary symbols, thus :

$$\begin{align*}
& 2^{\sigma-1} D s=\sigma \frac{d^{\sigma-1}}{d x^{\sigma-1}} \frac{d}{d y}-\frac{\sigma(\sigma-1)(\sigma-2)}{1.2 \cdot 3} \frac{d^{\sigma-3}}{d x^{\sigma-3}} \frac{d^{3}}{d y^{3}}+\text { \&c. }  \tag{60}\\
& 2^{\sigma-1} D c=\frac{d^{\sigma}}{d x^{\sigma}}-\frac{\sigma(\sigma-1) d^{\sigma-2}}{1.2} \frac{d^{2}}{d x^{\sigma-2}} \frac{d y^{2}}{d y^{2}}+\text { \&c. } \tag{61}
\end{align*}$$


We shall albo write

$$\begin{equation*}
\frac{d^{n-\sigma}}{d z^{n-\sigma}} D_{s}^{(\sigma)}=D_{s}^{(\sigma)}, \text { and } \quad \frac{d^{n-\sigma}}{d z^{n-\sigma}} D_{c}^{(\sigma)}=D_{n}^{(\sigma)} ; \tag{62}
\end{equation*}$$

so that $\underset{n}{(\sigma)}$ and $\underset{n}{\stackrel{(\sigma)}{\nu}}$ denote the operations of differentiating with
 respect to $n$ axes, $n-\sigma$ of which coincide with the axis of $z$,
 while the remaining $\sigma$ make equal angles with each other in the
 plane of $x y, D_{\underset{n}{(\sigma)}}$ being used when the axis of $y$ coincides with
 one of the axes, and $D_{n}^{c}$ when the axis of $y$ bisects the angle
 between two of the axes.


The two tesseral surface harmonics of order $n$ and type $\sigma$ may
 now be written

$$\begin{align*}
Y_{n}^{(\sigma)} & =(-1)^{n} \frac{1}{n!} r^{n+1} D_{n}^{(\sigma)} \frac{1}{r},  \tag{63}\\
Y_{n}^{(\sigma)} & =(-1)^{n} \frac{1}{n!} r^{n+1} D_{n}^{(\sigma)} \frac{1}{r} . \tag{64}
\end{align*}$$


Writing $\mu=\cos \theta, \quad \nu=\sin \theta, \quad \rho^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}, \quad r^{2}=\xi \eta+z^{2}$,
 so that $\quad z=\mu r, \quad \rho=\nu r, \quad x=\rho \cos \phi, \quad y=\rho \sin \phi$,

we have

$$\begin{equation*}
D_{\delta}^{(\sigma)} \frac{1}{\gamma}=(-1)^{\sigma} \frac{(2 \sigma)!}{2^{2 \sigma} \sigma!} i\left(\eta^{\sigma}-\xi^{\sigma}\right) \frac{1}{r^{2 \sigma+1}}, \tag{65}
\end{equation*}$$

$$\begin{equation*}
D_{c}^{(\sigma)} \frac{1}{\gamma^{\prime}}=(-1)^{\sigma} \frac{(2 \sigma)!}{2^{2 \sigma} \sigma!}\left(\xi^{\sigma}+\eta^{\sigma}\right) \frac{1}{\gamma^{2 \sigma+1}}, \tag{66}
\end{equation*}$$

in which we may write

$$\begin{equation*}
\frac{i}{2}\left(\eta^{\sigma}-\xi^{\sigma}\right)=\rho^{\sigma} \sin \sigma \phi, \quad \frac{1}{2}\left(\xi^{\sigma}+\eta^{\sigma}\right)=\rho^{\sigma} \cos \sigma \phi . \tag{67}
\end{equation*}$$


We have now only to differentiate with respect to $z$, which we
 may do so as to obtain the result either in terms of $p$ and $z$, or as
 a homogeneous function of $z$ and $\rho$ divided by a power of $r$,

$$\begin{align*}
d z^{n-\sigma} \frac{1}{r^{2 \sigma+1}}=(-1)^{n-\sigma} \frac{(2 n)!}{2^{n} n!} \frac{2^{\sigma} \sigma!}{(2 \sigma)!} \frac{1}{r^{2 n+1}} \times & \\
& {\left[z^{n-\sigma}-\frac{(n-\sigma)(n-\sigma-1)}{2(2 n-1)} z^{n-\sigma-2} r^{2}+\& \mathrm{c} .\right] * } \tag{68}
\end{align*}$$

$$\text { or } \begin{align*}
\frac{d^{n-\sigma}}{d z^{n-\sigma}} \frac{1}{r^{2 \sigma+1}}= & (-1)^{n-\sigma} \frac{(n+\sigma)!}{(2 \sigma)!} 1 \\
& {\left[z^{n-\sigma}-\frac{(n-\sigma)(n-\sigma-1)}{4(\sigma+1)} z^{n-\sigma-2} \rho^{2}+\& \mathrm{c} .\right] } \tag{69}
\end{align*}$$


If we write

$$\begin{align*}
\Theta_{n}^{(\sigma)} & =\nu^{\sigma}\left[\mu^{n-\sigma}-\frac{(n-\sigma)(n-\sigma-1)}{2(2 n-1)} \mu^{n-\sigma-2}\right. \\
& \left.+\frac{(n-\sigma)(n-\sigma-1)(n-\sigma-2)(n-\sigma-3)}{2 \cdot 4(2 n-1)(2 n-3)} \mu^{n-\sigma-4}-\& \mathrm{c} .\right] \tag{70}
\end{align*}$$

and

$$\begin{align*}
& \mathcal{L}_{n}^{(\sigma)}=\nu^{\sigma}\left[\mu^{n-\sigma}-\frac{(n-\sigma)(n-\sigma-1)}{4(\sigma+1)} \mu^{n-\sigma-2} \nu^{2}\right. \\
& \left.\quad+\frac{(n-\sigma)(n-\sigma-1)(n-\sigma-2)(n-\sigma-3)}{4.8(\sigma+1)(\sigma+2)} \mu^{n-\sigma-4} \nu^{4}-\& \mathrm{c} .\right] \tag{71}
\end{align*}$$

then

$$\begin{equation*}
\Theta_{n}^{(\sigma)}=\frac{2^{n-\sigma} n!(n+\sigma)!}{(2 n)!\sigma!} L_{n}^{(\sigma)}, \tag{72}
\end{equation*}$$

so that these two functions differ only by a constant factor.

We may now write the expressions for the two tesseral har
monics of order $n$ and type $\sigma$ in terms either of $\Theta$ or $\mathscr{I}$,

$$\begin{align*}
Y_{n}^{(\sigma)} & =\frac{(2 n)!}{2^{n+\sigma} n!n!} \Theta_{n}^{(\sigma)} 2 \sin \sigma \phi=\frac{(n+\sigma)!}{2^{2 \sigma} n!\sigma!} \Omega_{n}^{(\sigma)} 2 \sin \sigma \phi  \tag{73}\\
Y_{n}^{(\sigma)} & =\frac{(2 n)!}{2^{n+\sigma} n!n!} \Theta_{n}^{(\sigma)} 2 \cos \sigma \phi=\frac{(n+\sigma)!}{2^{2 \sigma} n!\sigma!} \Omega_{n}^{(\sigma)} 2 \cos \sigma \phi \dagger \tag{74}
\end{align*}$$

