then we have to determine the constants of the forms $h$ and $k$ so
 that when $r=a(1+F), \Psi=a$, and when $r=b(1+G), \Psi=\beta$.


It is manifest, from our former investigation, that all the $h$ 's
 and $k$ 's except $h_{0}$ and $k_{0}$ will be small quantities, the products of
 which into $F$ may be neglected. We may, therefore, write

$$\begin{align*}
& a=h_{0}+k_{0} \frac{1}{a}(1-F)+\text { \&c. }+\left(h_{n}^{(\sigma)} u^{n}+k_{n}^{(\sigma)} \frac{1}{a^{n+1}}\right) Y_{n}^{(\sigma)}+\cdots  \tag{22}\\
& \beta=h_{0}+k_{0} \frac{1}{b}(1-G)+\text { \&c. }+\left(h_{n}^{(\sigma)} b^{n}+k_{n}^{(\sigma)} \frac{1}{b^{n+1}}\right) Y_{n}^{(\sigma)}+\cdots \tag{23}
\end{align*}$$


We have therefore

$$\begin{align*}
a & =h_{0}+k_{0} \frac{1}{a}  \tag{24}\\
\beta & =h_{0}+k_{0} \frac{1}{b}  \tag{25}\\
k_{0} \frac{1}{a} f_{n}^{(\sigma)} & =h_{n}^{(\sigma)} u^{n}+k_{n}^{(\sigma)} \frac{1}{a^{n+1}}  \tag{26}\\
k_{0} \frac{1}{b} g_{n}^{(\sigma)} & =h_{n}^{(\sigma)} b^{n}+k_{n}^{(\sigma)} \frac{1}{b^{n+1}} \tag{27}
\end{align*}$$

whence we find for $k_{0}$, the charge of the inner conductor,

$$\begin{equation*}
k_{0}=(\alpha-\beta) \frac{a b}{b-a} \tag{28}
\end{equation*}$$

and for the coefficients of the harmonics of order $n$

$$\begin{align*}
& h_{n}^{(\sigma)}=k_{0} \frac{b^{n} g_{n}^{(\sigma)}-a^{n} f_{n}^{(\sigma)}}{b^{2 n+1}-a^{2 n+1}}  \tag{29}\\
& k_{n}^{(\sigma)}=k_{0} a^{n} b^{n} \frac{b^{n+1} f_{n}^{(\sigma)}-a^{n+1} g_{n}^{(\sigma)}}{b^{2 n+1}-a^{2 n+1}} \tag{30}
\end{align*}$$

where we must remember that the coefficients $f_{n}^{(\sigma)}, g_{n}^{(\tau)}, h_{n}^{(\sigma)}, k_{n}^{(\sigma)}$ are
 those belonging to the same type as well as order.


The surface density on the inner conductor is given by the
 equation

$$\begin{equation*}
4 \pi \sigma a^{2}=k_{0}\left(1+\ldots+A_{n} Y_{n}^{(\sigma)}+\ldots\right) \tag{31}
\end{equation*}$$

where $A_{n}=\frac{f_{n}^{(\sigma)}\left\{(n+2) a^{2 n+1}+(n-1) b^{2 n+1}\right\}-g_{n}^{(\sigma)}(2 n+1) a^{n+1} b^{n}}{b^{2 n+1}-a^{2 n+1}}$.

146.] As an example of the application of zonal harmonics,
 let us investigate the equilibrium of electricity on two spherical
 conductors.

Similarly, if the potential of the second sphere is $\beta$, for points
 within that sphere,

$$\begin{equation*}
U+V^{\prime}=\beta . \tag{8}
\end{equation*}$$


For points outside both spheres the potential is $\Psi$, where

$$\begin{equation*}
U+V=\Psi . \tag{9}
\end{equation*}$$


On the axis, between the centres of the spheres,

$$\begin{equation*}
r+8=c . \tag{10}
\end{equation*}$$


Hence, differentiating with respect to $r$, and after differentiation
 making $r=0$, and remembering that at the pole each of the
 zonal harmonics is unity, we find

$$\left.\begin{array}{l}
A_{1} \frac{1}{a^{2}}-\frac{d V}{d s}=0,  \tag{11}\\
A_{2} \frac{2!}{a^{3}}+\frac{d^{2} V}{d s^{2}}=0, \\
A_{m} \frac{m!}{a^{m+1}}+(-1)^{m} \frac{d^{m} V}{d s^{m}}=0,
\end{array}\right\}$$

where, after differentiation, $s$ is to be made equal to $c$.

If we perform the differentiations, and write $\alpha / c=x$ and
 $b / c=y$, these equations become

$$\left.\begin{array}{r}
0=A_{1}+B x^{2}+2 B_{1} x^{2} y+3 B_{2} x^{2} y^{2}+\text { \&c. }+(n+1) B_{n} x^{2} y^{n}  \tag{12}\\
0=A_{2}+B x^{3}+3 B_{1} x^{3} y+6 B_{2} x^{3} y^{2}+\text { \&c. }+\frac{1}{2}(n+1)(n+2) B_{n} x^{3} y^{n} \\
0=A_{m}+B x^{m+1}+(m+1) B_{1} x^{m+1} y+\frac{1}{2}(m+1)(m+2) B_{2} x^{m+1} y^{2} \\
+ \text { \&c. }+\frac{(m+n)!}{m!n!} B_{n} x^{m+1} y^{n}
\end{array}\right\}$$


By the corresponding operations for the second sphere we find,

$$\begin{array}{r}
0=B_{1}+A y^{2}+2 A_{1} x y^{2}+3 A_{2} x^{2} y^{2}+\text { \&c. }+(m+1) A_{m} x^{m} y^{2},  \tag{13}\\
0=B_{2}+A y^{3}+3 A_{1} x y^{3}+6 A_{2} x^{2} y^{3}+\text { \&c. }+\frac{1}{2}(m+1)(m+2) A_{m} x^{m} y^{3}, \\
\left.\begin{array}{r}
0=B_{n}+A y^{n+1}+(n+1) A_{1} x y^{n+1}+\frac{1}{2}(n+1)(n+2) A_{2} x^{2} y^{n+1}+\text { \&c. } \\
+\frac{(m+n)!}{m!n!} A_{m} x^{m} y^{n+1}
\end{array}\right\}
\end{array}$$


To determine the potentials, $a$ and $\beta$, of the two spheres we
 have the equations (7) and (8), which we may now write

$$\begin{align*}
& c a=A \frac{1}{x}+B+B_{1} y+B_{2} y^{2}+\text { \&c. }+B_{n} y^{n}  \tag{14}\\
& c \beta=B \frac{1}{y}+A+A_{1} x+A_{2} x^{2}+\text { \&c. }+A_{m} x^{m} . \tag{15}
\end{align*}$$
